{"title":"Jadhav Division Axiom","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p><b>Jadhav Division Axiom</b> is a method of predicting the number of digits before decimal point in a common fraction, derived by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Jyotiraditya_Jadhav&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Jyotiraditya Jadhav (page does not exist)\">Jyotiraditya Jadhav</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Statement\">Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Jadhav_Division_Axiom&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For any fraction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27f583697295fc6f723862c13228ee55107c33ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{m}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"22\" height=\"34\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4c2c1b6573843d0f14dfa37121402755a93b4e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\cdot 10^{k-1} &lt; m &lt; n \\cdot 10^{k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"184\" height=\"18\" />, when expressed as a decimal, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> digits before the decimal point.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Uses\">Uses</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Jadhav_Division_Axiom&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Uses\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>All types of division processes</li>\n<li>Can be used to correctly predict the nature of the answer for long division processes.</li>\n<li>Can be used to determine the sine and cosine functions of extreme angles</li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Jadhav_Division_Axiom&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"background:#ffdddd; width:60%; border:2px solid #aa0000; margin:auto; text-align:center;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;\">\n<p><b>This page has been proposed for <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AoPS_Wiki:Deletion_policy\" title=\"AoPS Wiki:Deletion policy\">deletion</a>.</b> Reason: <b>axiom is redundant (can be proven from other theorems) and is not well-known.</b>\n</p>\n<hr style=\"border:1px solid #aa0000;\" />\n<p><small>\n<b>Note to sysops:</b> Before deleting, please review: • <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Special:WhatLinksHere/Jadhav_Division_Axiom\" title=\"Special:WhatLinksHere/Jadhav Division Axiom\">What links here</a> • <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Talk:Jadhav_Division_Axiom&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Talk:Jadhav Division Axiom (page does not exist)\">Discussion page</a> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Jadhav_Division_Axiom&amp;action=history\">Edit history</a>\n</small>\n</p>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018092043\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.017 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.031 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 38/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1152/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 77/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 567/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.561      1 -total\n 56.27%    7.630      1 Template:Delete\n 42.65%    5.784      1 Templa